i'm joined today by iraq war veteran and  Purple Heart recipient will Fisher who's  the executive director of the Union  veterans council through afl-cio he's  been traveling the country talking to  veterans and other voters about what a  Donald Trump presidency would mean for  veterans and military families and I  want to start of will by getting suit  your reaction to the caricature we often  see the idea of Republican strong on the  military democrat week when it comes to  the military is that even anything we  should be remotely paying attention to  in evaluating policy with regard to our  veterans anymore or has that gone out  the window I think it really is  something that never really existed so a  lot of that was born out of you know at  the end of the Vietnam War and I believe  that you know a lot of people saw  especially in the early 80s through you  know some of the grandstanding done by  President Reagan and some of the pomp  and circumstance around the military  that somehow being strong in the fence  meant that you were strong and stood  with veterans which is absolutely not  the case I mean right now do you have  the Republicans in Congress who are  trying to destroy and privatize the VA  for instance you have Republicans in  Congress who want to do away with the GI  Bill right you have Republicans in  Congress we want to cut pay for active  duty military and it extends of course  well beyond the actions of the Congress  because right now we have in a the  republican party's nominee for president  united states in Donald Trump someone  who look very rarely embraces any policy  any policy when it comes to veterans and  military families and our active service  and when he does it's things like doing  away with the GI Bill it's things like  seni wants to destroy and privatize the  VA it's things like speaking in a very  charlatan manner  or a very Cavalier manner about using  nuclear weapons but it goes so much  deeper than that with mr. Trump you know  this is someone who is also spent a year  on the campaign trail attacking our  military insulting veterans saying that  people like John McCain and other  prisoners of war are losers because they  were captured someone who compares their  sex life in the 80s and 90s to serving  in combat in Vietnam someone who says  that when young women and men get him by  roadside bombs I just go for a little  ride in the air someone who attacks gold  star families I mean where are we in our  politics where we have somebody out  there in this case it's republican  party's nominee for president attacking  gold star families people have already  sacrificed more than many can even begin  to imagine well a lot of signs uh in  thinking of of this sort of line of  thinking that you're that you're talking  talking to us about this comment that  Donald Trump made which he's now chalked  up to some kind of joke which was he's  always wanted a purple heart and he was  handed a physical one by a veteran at an  event some time ago uh as you know we  don't have to speculate about what that  meant to a Purple Heart recipient we can  actually just ask you did you find that  offensive or did you find it to be the  light-hearted joke that Donald Trump  claimed it to be once people said hey  you know you might want to block that  back you know I think that what happened  is someone asked me the exact same  question you just now did about 10  minutes after mr. Trump made those  comments and I was so struck by what he  said that I immediately just said the  first thing that came to mind for me and  the first thing came to mind for me when  I heard Donald Trump said he had always  wanted a purple heart was all this in  nineteen 2004 you see on that day  outside of the city of Fallujah I was  awarded a Purple Heart but so was my  Marine Corps brother who got his arm  blown off so was my Marine Corps brother  who lost Anna and so was my Marine Corps  brother who was killed that day and he  didn't when they will receive his Purple  Heart his widow had to receive it on his  behalf and you know I guess what Donald  didn't learn  in that elite military prep school he  attended is that purple hearts are  reminders of very bad days and nobody  nobody out there that has one ever  wanted it you know and I think it's also  important to remember that this is  somebody who makes these comments about  veterans who makes these comments about  our military who categorically at the  same time wants to send more young  American women and men in the combat but  it won't be his kids right I don't think  anybody thinks that Donnie jr. Eric or  vodka are gonna put on a flak jacket  anytime soon but if Donald Trump is  elected president the people out there  listening today their kids will and the  people who they work with their kids  will in the people that they worship  with the participating community  activities with their kids will and it's  mighty bold talk mighty bold talk come  from somebody who died service uh when  it was their time to serve with the help  of multiple deferments and having a Rich  Dad well along those lines you know I  this is a little bit of a departure from  trump specifically and and it's more  just a commentary on what we started  with which is this idea of I support the  troops you don't support the troops as  president here's how I would support the  troops and very very often that that  becomes a phrase that not only lacks  meaning but it actually sometimes takes  on opposite meaning and very very often  supporting the troops is is uh wrapped  up with uh sending troops into  engagements that are sometimes primarily  sometimes partially motivated by  political gain and political posturing  and I feel like that is not discussed  enough the idea that the number one way  to really value the volunteer service  all of our our troops is not to ever use  troops for political battles or in areas  where it is not absolutely necessary and  I feel like that frank conversation is  not had enough I don't know how you feel  about that absolutely I think that one  thing that is important to understand  that we always try to focus on is  lifting up the fact that the veteran  class and the working  s are one in the same that the veteran  class and the working class are one in  the same into your point look make no  mistake about it right make no mistake  about it the exact same people who are  more than willing to send the working  class all over this world to fight wars  with our blood are very often the exact  same people who are more than willing to  amass fortunes up the sweat and labor of  veterans once we get back home you know  somebody it's easy for a politician to  stand up in a truck bed or on the stage  giving a speech and talk about how much  they support veterans I mean anybody can  say that I support the troops I want to  honor the sacrifice of troops and  veterans look I put them in my TV  commercials etc but then how often are  those exact same people who we hear so  loudly talk about how much they support  veterans the exact same people who work  day in and day out to do things like  destroy and privatize the VA to get rid  of veterans job training and education  benefits like the GI Bill but also do  not veterans on the ability to have a  voice on the job the ability to  collectively bargain and have access to  a career to a good job that allows a  veteran to live with life with dignity  and live out the American dream that we  fought to protect and defend you know  then that's something that I think very  important also in this race for the  presidency Hillary Clinton does not view  veterans or the working class as  political props rather she looks as  human beings right that's why his first  lady it was Hillary Clinton new chimp  was raising awareness around issues  affecting Gulf War veterans that's why I  was cinder Hillary Clinton who was  championing legislation like the 21st  century GI bill and it will be president  Hillary Clinton will work to expand  veterans job training benefits education  benefits and under no circumstances  allow our VA to be destroyed and  privatized and even deeper than that  going back to a point I just now made  Secretary Clinton fundamentally  understands that what a veteran needs  access to more than anything upon  returning home is not a yellow ribbon  it's not a standing ovation at a  football game  what a veteran needs access to more than  anything is a good job in a good job  allows that person live a life with  dignity will in the limited time we have  left uh yesterday i was watching this  town hall or recently i was watching  this town hall that president obama did  with members of the military and i  picked up on some pretty significant  skepticism from many of the of the  troops in the room who clearly were  being respectful to the president but  they had questions that were if not  adversarial certainly evidence that they  were very skeptical of the president's  policies not specifically about this  town hall but generally what I'm curious  to hear from you is what types of  statements from politicians about troops  veterans the military etc are concerning  type statements for you where you say  you know that I'm hearing a talking  point that is a talking point that is is  concerning to me and what types of  statements from politicians are the ones  that you are more likely to stay here as  though that could be a good thing mm-hmm  I think it's a great question i think  the ones that make me the most wary are  very often the ones that you hear from  these folks that are always banging  banging the drum for more war when they  use their quote unquote support for  troops as a an opportunity to make  inroads into talking about going into  more wars and creating more veterans  right and i think the ones that the  conversations that veterans are  interested in conversations veterans  want to have go back to those class  issues what r is this elected official  doing to enable me to join with my  fellow veterans to join with my fellow  you know working class neighbors and  colleagues to build out a better life  for ourselves and live out the kind of  life that that we want that we need and  that every person should be entitled to  i think that when you look at Donald  Trump  this is someone who Tom and Tom again  has shown that he has no real plans  whatsoever you hear him talking about  the military you hear him talking about  veterans and what does he talk about he  says our military is full of losers  right he I don't even need to rehash all  of the anti veteran statements that he's  made thus far even though there are  plenty more in that reservoir this is  someone who also alienate our allies on  a daily basis this is someone who also  for some weird reason has this obsession  including in the debate to the night of  seizing every opportunity he can to fan  dance for a you know an authoritarian  dictator in Vladimir Putin Donald Trump  is categorically unprepared to be  President of the United States he's  mendacious he's capricious he's a  chickenhawk charlatan and he knows  nothing of what veterans and the working  class in this country face and he has no  plan to bring about the changes that we  need Hillary Clinton on the other hand  is aptly prepared to be Prezi nited  states a commander-in-chief she has a  plan to keep Americans safe she has  appertain a plan to defeat our enemies  and a plan to bring people together to  make those things happen and Hillary  Clinton also as a plan and has a track  record of standing with veterans and  with the working class and that will  continue long after she's occupying the  Oval Office we have been speaking with  Iraq war veteran and Purple Heart  recipient will Fisher executive director  of the Union veterans council through  afl-cio will thanks so much for talking  to me today I can't thank you enough i  look forward to doing it again sometime
 

